WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.735
A lens describes a permitted view of data. A permission policy decides who may receive

00:00:05.735 --> 00:00:11.070
that view or perform an action. We build those decisions from the same predicate

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:16.204
language. Here, an organization must be active and on the Pro plan. Each test

00:00:16.204 --> 00:00:21.939
evaluates information about the organization. The all group requires both to pass. The

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:27.475
resulting decision determines whether that organization receives the platform lens.

00:00:31.975 --> 00:00:37.172
An attribute is simply a property of something: whether a relationship is active,

00:00:37.172 --> 00:00:42.562
which plan an organization has, or which region it belongs to. A permission rule can

00:00:42.562 --> 00:00:47.503
test those properties just as a data query tests a shipment’s status. That is

00:00:47.503 --> 00:00:52.701
attribute-based access control. The name sounds specialized, but the mechanism is

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:58.219
familiar: evaluate a condition against the relevant data, then use the result to allow

00:00:58.219 --> 00:00:59.375
or deny an action.

00:01:03.875 --> 00:01:09.320
A role gives a named set of capabilities, such as viewer or analyst. A relationship

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:14.830
describes how one record is connected to another, such as a customer’s membership in

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:20.341
an organization. Here, the policy follows the customer relationship and requires the

00:01:20.341 --> 00:01:25.786
reader action. An analyst can inherit that action. Combining attributes, roles, and

00:01:25.786 --> 00:01:31.166
relationships lets the policy express both who someone is in this context and what

00:01:31.166 --> 00:01:32.675
they are allowed to do.

00:01:37.175 --> 00:01:42.970
Sofia has access through two organizations. The evaluated catalog shows the lens each

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:48.697
organization makes available to her. An agent can ask which lenses it may use before

00:01:48.697 --> 00:01:54.356
asking for data across them. This predicate asks for delayed records. Each selected

00:01:54.356 --> 00:01:59.606
lens is checked separately, and each query retains its own identity bindings.

00:01:59.606 --> 00:02:05.197
Combining the answers does not combine the organizations into an unrestricted data

00:02:05.197 --> 00:02:05.675
source.

00:02:10.175 --> 00:02:15.791
Now change Pacific’s plan from Pro to Starter. The same policy evaluates the new value

00:02:15.791 --> 00:02:21.342
and denies access, so its lens disappears from Sofia’s catalog. These definitions and

00:02:21.342 --> 00:02:26.762
their inputs are dynamic data. They can be serialized and persisted; they are not a

00:02:26.762 --> 00:02:31.987
deployment-time configuration file. In this demo the change takes effect without

00:02:31.987 --> 00:02:37.407
redeploying the application. Earlier results are hidden, and the next allowed query

00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:38.975
uses the remaining lens.

00:02:43.475 --> 00:02:48.674
An agent may remember an old lens identifier, but remembering it is not permission to

00:02:48.674 --> 00:02:53.934
use it. Replaying the old identifier now fails. Changing Mateo’s role also changes the

00:02:53.934 --> 00:02:58.705
evaluated decision under a policy that requires analyst access. This is access

00:02:58.705 --> 00:03:03.476
evaluation and revocation. Rule staleness is a separate question: did upstream

00:03:03.476 --> 00:03:08.308
narrowing make the predicate itself invalid? The application needs both checks,

00:03:08.308 --> 00:03:11.550
because valid syntax alone does not establish access.
